## The instrument had to be built first

🔴 **`sflowtool` is not installed on tb277 and installing it is root (STANDING-ORDERS §4), and
`tools/` had no sFlow decoder.** So this case begins by writing one: **`tools/sflowwire.py`**,
on the model of `tools/snmpwire.py` -- pure Python, no scapy, reading a `tcpdump -w` pcap and
reusing `snmpwire.py`'s classic-pcap reader and Ethernet/802.1Q/IPv4/UDP walk. It prints, per
datagram, the agent address, sub-agent id, datagram sequence number, agent uptime and sample
count; per flow sample the sampling rate, sample pool, drops, input and output ifIndex, and the
sampled frame's own header decoded (MACs, VLAN tags, EtherType, IPv4 addresses, protocol,
ports); per counters sample the ifIndex and the generic interface counters. Options `--abs`
(absolute timestamps), `--quiet` (SUMMARY only) and **`--find TEXT`** (count the sampled headers
whose bytes contain an ASCII marker -- which is how STEP 7 below proves *which* burst a sample
belongs to, using `vsend.py --tag`).

**It was self-tested offline before it was trusted**: a synthetic sFlow v5 datagram was built by
hand in the session scratchpad (one flow sample with a raw-header record of a known 86-byte
frame, plus one counters sample with generic interface counters) and written into a hand-made
pcap. The decoder returned the agent, sub-agent, sequence, uptime, `sampling_rate=1-in-50`,
`input=5010`, and the frame's MACs, addresses and ports exactly as constructed. Only then was it
pointed at the DUT. It is documented in `tools/README.md`.

