## STEP 4 (designed) -- the agent transmits, and the datagram says what it was told to say

100 s capture of udp/6343 with a 2000-frame burst in the middle (`work/s6-measure.log`,
decode in `work/s7-decode.log`, 22 datagrams):

    10:57:37  tcpdump -i eth3 -w s6on.pcap udp port 6343   (100 s)
    10:58:29  2000 frames, --tag CK44309ON   (14.07 s)
    10:59:18  capture closed -- 22 packets captured

    SUMMARY 22 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 40 flow sample(s), 5 counters sample(s);
            agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 4..25

The first datagram, in full:

    #1   10:57:50.545 10.38.22.34:58995 -> 10.38.22.33:6343  sFlow v5  agent=10.38.22.34
         sub_agent=0 seq=4 uptime=315279000ms samples=1
        counters sample seq=3 source=0:5010
          2 counter record(s)
          record format 2, 52 bytes (not decoded)
          if-counters: ifIndex=5010 ifType=6 ifSpeed=1000000000 ifDirection=1 ifStatus=0x3
            in:  octets=1223665 ucast=10119 multicast=2730 broadcast=115 discards=0 errors=0 unknownproto=0
            out: octets=42450777 ucast=315886 multicast=168166 broadcast=760 discards=0 errors=0

Every claim the case needs, measured:

| what | measured |
| --- | --- |
| the datagrams are sFlow **v5** | yes, all 22 |
| the **agent address** is the configured one | `agent=10.38.22.34` on all 22 -- and note that is the DUT's vlan10 address, which was configured explicitly, not inferred |
| **sub-agent id** | 0 on all 22 |
| **datagram sequence** | 4..25, contiguous, no gap |
| the **destination** is the configured collector | 10.38.22.33:6343 on all 22 |
| **flow samples** | **40** -- from 2000 frames at a configured 1-in-50, i.e. exactly 2000/50 |
| the **sampling rate the datagram reports** | `sampling_rate=1-in-50` on **all 40**, the configured value |
| the **sampled port** | `input=5010` on 39 of 40 (`input=0` on one, see below); port1.0.10 is the only port with a sampling rate |
| the **sampled frames are mine** | 38 of the 40 sampled headers decode to `dst=02:00:00:00:44:09 src=00:00:5e:00:53:01 ethertype=0x0800 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.40 proto=UDP dport=9` -- the exact frame `vsend.py` was told to build |
| **sample_pool** | advances by exactly 50 per sample (100, 150, 200, 250, 300 ...), i.e. the configured rate |
| **flow sample sequence** | 2..41, 40 consecutive distinct sampling events |
| **counters samples** | 5 in 100 s, i.e. one per 20 s -- the configured polling interval |
| the **polled port** | `ifIndex=5010` on **all 5**, and port1.0.10 is the only port with a polling interval, out of 77 ports on the unit. That is the per-port scope of polling, measured |

**40 samples for 2000 frames at 1-in-50 is the central measurement of this case** and it is
exact. **STEP 4 PASS.**

Two small notes, neither a finding: one flow sample carries `input=0` (no ingress interface --
a frame the switch itself originated), and the counters sample carries a second record of
format 2 (the ethernet/dot3Stats counters) that `sflowwire.py` does not decode; its generic
format-1 record is decoded in full. The tool's README says so.

## STEP 5 (designed) -- change only the rate, and the yield changes with it

`sflow sampling-rate 500` on port1.0.10, nothing else touched
(`show sflow interface | include port1.0.10` -> `port1.0.10  500  128  20`). The **identical**
2000-frame burst (`work/s8-rate500.log`):

    SUMMARY 7 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 3 flow sample(s), 4 counters sample(s);
            agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 28..34
    all 3 flow samples report sampling_rate=1-in-500

**40 samples became 3** for the same 2000 frames when the rate went from 1-in-50 to 1-in-500,
and every sample reports the new rate. 2000/500 = 4 expected; 3 observed, which is what "the
mean sampling rate N" means -- it is a probabilistic 1-in-N, not a counter. Counters samples
continued at the unchanged 20 s interval. **This is the quantitative control that the rate is
honoured and not merely reported.** **STEP 5 PASS.**

## STEP 6 (designed) -- disable, and the first attempt at the removal check

    awplus(config)# no sflow enable

`show sflow` -> `sFlow Admin Status ........ Disabled` and
`Polling/sampling/Tx ....... Inactive because: - sFlow is disabled`.

Then the identical 2000-frame burst inside a 75 s capture (`work/s9-disabled.log`):

    11:01:47  tcpdump -i eth3 -w s9off.pcap udp port 6343   (75 s)
    11:02:13 - 11:02:27  2000 frames, --tag CK44309OFF2
    11:03:08  capture closed -- 11 packets captured

### 🔥 FINDING (product) -- sFlow kept sampling and transmitting after `no sflow enable`

    SUMMARY 11 sFlow v5 datagram(s): 85 flow sample(s), 0 counters sample(s);
            agent(s) 10.38.22.34; seq 36..46

**Eleven sFlow datagrams carrying 85 flow samples were transmitted to the collector after the
operator disabled sFlow and after the CLI confirmed it was disabled.** The detail, all from the
decode:

- **The samples are of traffic that arrived AFTER the disable.** `sflowwire.py --find`:
  **78 of the 85 sampled headers contain `CK44309OFF2`**, the payload marker of the burst sent
  25 seconds after `no sflow enable`. 0 contain `CK44309R500` or `CK44309ON`, the markers of the
  two earlier bursts. So they are not a stale buffer of old samples; the sampler sampled new
  frames.
- **They are 85 distinct sampling events**, not one buffer re-sent: the flow sample sequence
  numbers are **45..129**, consecutive, continuing the agent's own counter from the enabled legs.
- **The agent's own accounting is wrong too.** `sample_pool` steps by exactly 500 per sample --
  4050, 4550, 5050, ... 46050 -- so the agent believes 42,500 packets passed the sampler. At the
  measured transmission rate of the burst (2000 frames in 14.1 s) roughly **110 frames** had
  entered port1.0.10 by the time the last of these datagrams was sent.
- **All 11 arrived in a single 0.5-second burst**, 11:02:13.277 to 11:02:13.775, every one
  stamped with the same agent uptime (315542000 ms, which independently corroborates the capture
  clock: 10:57:50 + 263 s = 11:02:13). After that the capture stayed empty for its remaining 55
  seconds, although the frame burst ran on until 11:02:27.
- **The counters half DID stop**: zero counters samples in 75 s, against one every 20 s while
  enabled. So the disable took effect on polling and not on sampling.

**Why this matters beyond a conformance nit:** sFlow samples carry the headers of real user
traffic. A datagram emitted after an operator has switched monitoring off sends packet headers
to a collector that the operator believes is no longer receiving them.

## STEP 7 (designed) -- is it reproducible? Three faithful cycles say no

A finding reported once is worth much less than a finding with a trigger, so the removal check
was repeated three times, each closing one gap in the first observation.

| # | what was repeated | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 (`work/s12-repeat.log`) | sFlow left disabled for 5 minutes, then the identical 2000-frame burst, 120 s capture | **0 datagrams, 0 samples.** So it does not continue indefinitely |
| 2 (`work/s17-reproduction.log`) | a FRESH cycle: `sflow enable` with rate 500 -> burst (2 samples, both `CK44309ON2`) -> `no sflow enable` -> burst, 100 s capture | enabled leg correct; **0 datagrams after the disable** |
| 3 (`work/s19-reproduction2.log`) | the same again but with `sflow polling-interval 20` restored, so the configuration at the moment of disable was identical to the original | enabled leg correct (3 flow + 4 counters, all `CK44309ON3`); **0 datagrams after the disable** |
| 4 (`work/s21-reproduction3.log`) | the whole original sequence mirrored, including the **mid-flight rate change 50 -> 500 while enabled**, and the same ~28 s gap before the post-disable burst | burst B gave 4 samples, all `CK44309B`, at 1-in-500; **0 datagrams after the disable** |

⇒ **The behaviour was observed once and did not reproduce in three faithful enable -> disable
cycles, including one that mirrored the original sequence step for step.** It is reported as a
measured fact with its full evidence, and as *intermittent*. The pcap `/tmp/cksnmpmon/s9off.pcap`
is the record; it is on the box's tmpfs and will not survive a reboot, so the decode in this log
is the durable copy.

### ⚠️ A second inconsistency, found while reproducing

In reproduction 4's burst A -- `sflow sampling-rate 50` set while disabled, then `sflow enable`,
then the same 2000-frame burst -- the capture held **3 counters samples and ZERO flow samples**,
where STEP 4 had produced exactly 40 at the same rate with the same burst. The agent was
transmitting (the counters samples prove the path), the port's rate read 50, and no flow sample
appeared. Changing the rate to 500 then gave the expected 4.

So the flow-sample yield is **not always consistent with the configured rate across an
enable/disable cycle**: exact (40/40) on the first enable, zero on a later one at the same
setting. Recorded as an observation with its data; this run did not isolate it either, and
chasing it further would have been a rabbit hole in the last case of the group.

**Both anomalies are for a developer.** What a re-test should do: cycle `sflow enable` /
`no sflow enable` perhaps ten times with a counted burst and a capture in every window, rather
than the four cycles there was time for here.

